<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Front de libération du Québec</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FLQ, Quebec Liberation Front</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Front de libération du Québec was a militant separatist group that sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> independence through armed action. From 1963 it carried out bombings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> and elsewhere. In the October Crisis of 1970 its cells kidnapped the British diplomat James Cross and the Quebec minister Pierre Laporte, who was murdered, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-trudeau">Pierre Trudeau</a> invoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-measures-act">War Measures Act</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/front-de-liberation-du-quebec.jpg" alt="Front de libération du Québec" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_FLQ.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
